WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.720
Last spring, a couple friends of mine,&nbsp;
Jack and Hans, were having a disagreement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:10.040
and that disagreement was about buff spells. Jack&nbsp;
was insisting that they’re largely pretty bad and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.880
a waste of deck space in just about all formats,&nbsp;
whereas Hans was passionately defending them as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:21.120
an underrated type of card. After the conversation&nbsp;
was over, Hans, looking intensely off into space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.000
simply said “I need to make a buff spells&nbsp;
deck now.” And in the following weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.520
he did just that—he went for Temur colors, and&nbsp;
built it dirt cheap—as it turns out, buff spells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.960
are quite inexpensive, even the good ones. He&nbsp;
started with Pako and Haldan as the commanders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.120
which performed roughly as you’d expect for a $20&nbsp;
buff spells deck—it was a mediocre midrange deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:47.400
with some funky combat tricks, and Pako ate&nbsp;
his fair share of removal spells. But then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.480
Hans switched out the commander&nbsp;
for a much spicier choice—Xyris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:57.120
the Writhing Storm. Suddenly, the deck&nbsp;
started performing very differently.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.240
Games with this version of Xyris start off pretty&nbsp;
unassumingly—the deck typically plays a ramp spell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:07.520
and some sort of value creature turns 2 and 3, and&nbsp;
then plays its commander turn 4 or 5. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.080
it typically ramps a bit more, plays another&nbsp;
creature or two, and hits the person at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:16.360
table who is doing the worst. Combat step with&nbsp;
the Xyris deck generally sees Hans saying “who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:20.680
wants to draw some cards?” and then, as promised,&nbsp;
letting a person he’s okay with drawing cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:26.400
along with himself, draw cards. If a player kept&nbsp;
a risky hand and is getting flooded or screwed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:33.800
Xyris is here to save them, to bestow upon them&nbsp;
the gift of three, or six, or even nine cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:39.960
And this is the trap the deck lays—seeming like a&nbsp;
friend. I have seen a manascrewed player counter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:44.480
an opposing removal spell that targets Xyris&nbsp;
simply because they were desperate to keep drawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.760
those cards. The deck is friendly and unassuming&nbsp;
right up until the point where it decides to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.440
being those things and start murdering players.&nbsp;
Because fun fact—letting you and an opponent each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.840
draw 15 cards is great when that opponent&nbsp;
simultaneously dies to commander damage.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:04.800
Relevant to the way this deck operates in practice&nbsp;
is the Smol Bean Syndrome video I made awhile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.800
back. Hans plays the deck totally straight—he&nbsp;
doesn’t emphasize the deck’s power, nor its lack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:15.320
of power—but the deck nonetheless heavily plays&nbsp;
on the perceptions of its opponents. The deck is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:19.960
pretty predictable if you stop and take a moment&nbsp;
to think about it—it’s about 20% buff spells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:26.040
10% protection spells, 10% removal spells, and an&nbsp;
assortment of lands, ramp, and utility creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:30.600
from there. When you know how many cards the&nbsp;
deck has drawn, you can guess the approximate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:35.720
composition of its current hand—very little in&nbsp;
the deck is fully unique. And if you were to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:40.720
about this constantly, to realize that the deck&nbsp;
is probably currently holding multiple protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.440
spells, fight spells, and/or buff spells at a&nbsp;
given moment, and is going to get escalating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.680
numbers of these things on the coming turns, you&nbsp;
might get worried about what the deck is doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.280
and correctly so. But nobody seems to do this&nbsp;
sort of analysis—this deck gets underestimated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:01.040
game after game, and by people who have lost to it&nbsp;
half a dozen or more times. Even I frequently find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.880
myself underestimating the deck, specifically&nbsp;
because individual cards in the deck aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:11.920
very memorable. The deck’s lack of spicy plays and&nbsp;
absolute transparency in what it’s trying to do in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:17.600
a given game play to its strengths—it effortlessly&nbsp;
slides to the back of its opponents’ minds as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:23.600
that pile of bulk commons that will draw them some&nbsp;
cards, and it stays there until it’s far too late.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.840
I say far too late, because this deck more or&nbsp;
less operates on the snowball principle—early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:33.480
in the game it doesn’t have many buff spells,&nbsp;
protection spells, or removal spells. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:38.280
as it accumulates cards from Xyris hits it has&nbsp;
more and more resources to protect its gameplan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:42.880
and stop other people, and by the time it has&nbsp;
killed a player, it has access to basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.160
whatever resources from its deck that it wants. In&nbsp;
addition, the threat profile of the deck typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.560
lags behind its actual power—at any given moment,&nbsp;
people are usually concerned about the deck in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:59.280
amount they should have been concerned about it&nbsp;
a turn or two previously. However, despite not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.600
presenting as a deck the table should be worried&nbsp;
about winning, the deck does cultivate a specific,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:09.840
more mundane type of fear in its opponents, a fear&nbsp;
created directly from the act of running a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:15.960
buff spells. The combination of the deck being 20%&nbsp;
buff spells and it drawing a lot of cards gives an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.840
impression of an endless stream of buff spells.&nbsp;
When it comes down to it, the deck’s supply of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:26.760
buff spells is finite, it is spending resources to&nbsp;
use them, and it does run out of them sometimes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:31.120
But the deck’s opponents nonetheless learn to&nbsp;
exist in a constant state of fear during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:37.040
combat step—can I attack the Xyris player with my&nbsp;
5/5 trampler? No, they have a 1/1 Snake they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:42.600
block with, I don’t wanna lose my creature. Should&nbsp;
I block that Catgeist? Well no, of course not, do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:47.600
I look like somebody who throws my threats in the&nbsp;
garbage? This might seem like a minor factor, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.400
like many other aspects of the deck it’s a dynamic&nbsp;
that hinders its opponents’ abilities to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:58.000
together to slow it down. In theory if a player&nbsp;
is far ahead, you can make use of the multiplayer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.120
nature of the format—two or more opponents trading&nbsp;
cards one for one as blockers or through removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.760
spells will wear down a deck overtime. And if&nbsp;
anything this is more true in the case of opposing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:14.240
decks forcing Xyris to use buff spells outside of&nbsp;
dealing combat damage to a player—the deck’s happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:19.040
place is when it pours three to four buff spells&nbsp;
into killing a player with Xyris and restocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.160
its hand, and every buff spell it uses on damage&nbsp;
not being dealt to an opponent sets it back from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.400
getting to that place. But the impression&nbsp;
of a neverending tide of buff spells, paired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:34.800
with the deck’s delayed threat profile, keeps&nbsp;
opponents from playing this way in most games.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.960
The snakes Xyris creates bolster these&nbsp;
characteristics—both the way the deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.000
discourages its opponents from trying to engage&nbsp;
with it during the combat step and the snowball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:48.720
factor. Xyris generates a lot of snakes—even if&nbsp;
its opponents aren’t running draw of any kind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:53.560
it’s still generating as many snakes as the&nbsp;
commander deals damage. That means three per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:59.040
turn at minimum, and a dozen or more per turn&nbsp;
once the deck hits its stride. The result of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.720
compounds with the nature of being a buff spells&nbsp;
deck—the deck gains piles and piles of potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:08.120
attackers and blockers that could get larger&nbsp;
and gain all manner of potentially problematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:13.760
keywords at any moment. This also plays into the&nbsp;
snowball factor—once opponents catch onto how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:18.080
concerning the deck is, even if they manage to&nbsp;
dump enough removal into the commander to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.600
through the deck’s protection spells, they’re&nbsp;
still going to have a lot of snakes to deal with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:27.360
If there are enough of these snakes, they alone&nbsp;
could be adequate to push through for the win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.000
as the benefit of having a wide board of&nbsp;
attackers means that the deck can pile buff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:35.760
spells onto the unblocked snakes, effectively&nbsp;
creating a sort of evasion where the deck can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:41.320
decide to have a specific snake be its scary&nbsp;
attacker after blockers are already declared.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:45.800
The snake problem isn’t impossible to overcome,&nbsp;
though, and one good way to shut it down is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:49.800
play a board wipe. And actually, this can even&nbsp;
be a good way to deal with the deck’s commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:54.760
as well, since of its 11 cards that could be&nbsp;
considered protective, 5 of them are entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:00.520
useless against board wipes and the other 6 are&nbsp;
only situationally useful. So are board wipes this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:07.240
deck’s achilles heel? Kind of but not exactly, and&nbsp;
to explain why I need to talk about the ways decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:13.600
store accumulated resources. Most commander decks&nbsp;
tend to have some focus on building up resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:18.680
and a key question is what the deck does with&nbsp;
those resources to push its advantage but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:24.120
avoid losing them all. That key question’s&nbsp;
answer is the Ezuri-Kyler Scale of Accumulated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:29.960
Resource Permanency, trademark pending. Basically,&nbsp;
planeswalker emblems and experience counters are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:34.640
the most permanent type of resource as something&nbsp;
that opponents literally can’t interact with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:39.960
generally followed by lands, cards in hand,&nbsp;
a variety of different nonland permanents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:44.280
and a single type of nonland permanent, with&nbsp;
creatures whose presence on the board holds up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:51.480
a deck’s entire gameplan such as Kyler or Chainer&nbsp;
being the most vulnerable way to store resources.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:55.440
The Xyris buff spells deck is among&nbsp;
the decks that treat cards in hand as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:00.200
their primary place to store resources, but&nbsp;
these decks differ widely, with one axis of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:05.440
difference being especially key—velocity.&nbsp;
Control decks, combo decks, and a decent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:10.560
portion of high-curved midrange decks will place&nbsp;
high value on the stuff they have in their hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:15.480
but they generally play that stuff slowly—their&nbsp;
turnover of the stuff in their hand is pretty low,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:21.680
an attribute we could call being low velocity.&nbsp;
By contrast, Xyris buff spells is a very high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:26.080
velocity deck—buff spells can essentially&nbsp;
be thought of as creatures that cost only a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:31.200
fraction as much but stick around for a single&nbsp;
turn, and in later turns the deck will often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:36.880
churn through 5 or more of these per turn. The&nbsp;
result of this is that Xyris buff spells will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:41.640
somewhat resilient to board wipes, since most&nbsp;
of Xyris’s power is stored in the deck’s hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:46.280
and it can rapidly play them out the next turn&nbsp;
it has a creature to swing with. However, its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:51.280
high velocity also means that if the deck’s draw&nbsp;
is cut off multiple turns in a row, or if it loses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.920
Xyris at a moment where it’s already low on buff&nbsp;
spells, it will struggle to get back on its feet.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:00.720
With this weakness in mind, I feel&nbsp;
I should clarify what I mean when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.200
call this my playgroup’s best deck. This&nbsp;
isn’t a deck that generally dukes it out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:09.880
with high powered combo decks—it would&nbsp;
have a hard time in those games. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:14.440
this is a deck that gets pulled out in&nbsp;
low-medium power games—on paper it’s Hans’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:20.680
least powerful deck. And I think it shouldn’t go&nbsp;
understated just how unassuming this deck seems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:26.360
even having seen it in action—it’s mostly bulk&nbsp;
commons, it’s pretty dependent on the commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:31.120
it’s not very fast, the manabase is almost&nbsp;
entirely basics and taplands, and its core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:36.080
category of card is usually relegated to being&nbsp;
played in limited and maybe the fastest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:42.680
aggro decks. This deck doesn’t seem very good, and&nbsp;
that perception is exactly what makes it so good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:48.000
what causes it to win the vast majority of games&nbsp;
it’s featured in. The deck could absolutely be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:53.760
improved in terms of raw power level, especially&nbsp;
with another $20-30 in budget—it could run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:58.240
a couple more counterspells, it could run some&nbsp;
protective equipments, it could run better ramp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:03.560
and most importantly, it could pop in 2-3 cards&nbsp;
that give unlimited hand size, which would on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:09.680
paper eliminate one of its key weaknesses.&nbsp;
But here’s the thing—doing those things would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:15.040
turn this deck into a more generic Voltron deck,&nbsp;
one that is obviously threatening and is a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:20.600
visible ticking time bomb for everybody at the&nbsp;
table as long as Xyris is in play. I’ve argued in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:26.360
the past that Reliquary Tower is overrated, and&nbsp;
this is a strange case where it actually would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:31.240
be quite good from a power level standpoint,&nbsp;
and yet it’s still correct not to include it.

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:36.760
Discarding to hand size is the final dynamic&nbsp;
that causes people to underestimate the deck—if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:43.720
the deck has a fistful of ten or fifteen or even&nbsp;
twenty cards, it’s obviously a threat. But when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:49.320
you watch the deck discard back down to seven, you&nbsp;
have to ask yourself “come on, how good could that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:56.160
hand possibly be?” And in this case, you wouldn’t&nbsp;
even be wrong to ask that. There are only so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:01.280
protection spells and buff spells and other types&nbsp;
of spells that a seven card hand can contain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:06.600
and the deck does need to balance inflicting&nbsp;
lethal combat damage with protecting the commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:12.160
and retaining the ability to deal with opposing&nbsp;
threats. In this way, not running unlimited hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:16.800
size cards makes the deck more interesting&nbsp;
to play against, and also more interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:23.280
to play. In later turns the discard phase becomes&nbsp;
a critical decision-point, where the Xyris player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:27.840
must carefully plan out their resources and try&nbsp;
to navigate around what their opponents are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:32.960
to do. It’s like a turn-based boss fight where&nbsp;
the boss needs to carefully allot resources in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:38.400
order to best ward off the encroachments of the&nbsp;
other players—it’s honestly really cool, and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:43.760
kinda hard to ever be mad at the Xyris deck for&nbsp;
this reason even as it tends to pull off wins in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:49.640
the end regardless. Paradoxically, the Xyris buff&nbsp;
spells deck has the highest winrate of any deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:56.160
in my playgroup because of how dynamic and how&nbsp;
fair it is. The deck is a masterclass in playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:01.520
commander in the purest sense—it does a good job&nbsp;
of creating interesting gameplay for everybody at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:08.440
the table, and that interesting gameplay itself&nbsp;
greatly contributes to its ability to win games.

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:12.840
Thanks for watching! I had planned to release a&nbsp;
video about navigating power levels this week, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:17.200
I couldn’t quite get the script where I wanted it&nbsp;
in time, so I decided to make this video instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:22.000
You can expect that power level video next week,&nbsp;
though. I will also be posting an endnote to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:26.560
video tomorrow over on my Patreon that addresses&nbsp;
a particular deckbuilding style that was utilized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:31.200
in making this deck, so if you aren’t giving me&nbsp;
money yet, now is a pretty cool time to start!

